    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Assuming that the level of glucose is low, a mutation in the repressor associated with the lac operon of E. coli which prevents binding of the repressor to lactose should result in: \n\nOptions: \nA. expression of the genes only when glucose is present\nB. lack of expression or reduced expression of the lac operon genes under all circumstances\nC. no change in the expression of the lac operon genes\nD. expression of the genes only when lactose is absent\nE. constitutive expression of the lac operon genes\nF. expression of the genes only when lactose is present\nG. increased expression of the lac operon genes under all circumstances\nH. expression of the genes only when glucose is absent\nI. constitutive repression of the lac operon genes\nJ. reduced expression of the lac operon genes only when lactose is present\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The repressor protein in the *lac* operon normally binds to the operator to prevent transcription; lactose binding usually causes the repressor to detach, allowing expression. If a mutation prevents the repressor from binding to lactose (an \"I-s\" or super-repressor mutation), the repressor remains stuck to the operator regardless of lactose concentration. Therefore, the operon remains repressed at all times, leading to a lack of or reduced gene expression in every condition.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
