      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{A}.\n\nReducing the sample size decreases the power of a study (1 - beta), which mathematically results in an increase in the probability of a type II error (beta). A type II error is synonymous with a false negative result; therefore, both A and G represent the same statistical outcome. In the context of standard medical board examinations, \"Chance of a type II error\" is the most conventional phrasing for this phenomenon.",
