      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{I}.\n\nYersinia enterocolitica is a common pathogen that grows on Hektoen enteric agar (producing yellow/orange colonies) and can cause diarrhea following antibiotic exposure. While *Clostridium difficile* is a classic cause of post-antibiotic diarrhea, it does not grow on Hektoen enteric agar. *Salmonella* and *Shigella* also grow on this medium but are less associated with the specific clinical context of prolonged antibiotic treatment compared to the yersiniosis that can mimic appendicitis (pseudoappendicitis).",
