    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5443",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A healthy 22-year-old man participates in a study of glucose metabolism. At the beginning of the study, his serum glucose concentration is within the reference range. He consumes an 800-calorie meal consisting of protein, fat, and carbohydrates. He then sleeps through the night without additional food or drink. Twelve hours later, his serum glucose concentration remains within the reference range. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely involved in maintaining this man's serum glucose concentration?\n\nOptions: \nA. Increased glucose uptake by the brain\nB. Continued gut absorption of calories from the ingested meal\nC. Increased glucose uptake by red blood cells\nD. Glucose release from skeletal muscle\nE. Glycogenolysis in the liver\nF. Increased leptin release from adipose tissues\nG. Enhanced gluconeogenesis in the kidney\nH. Elevated insulin secretion from the pancreas\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Twelve hours after an 800-calorie meal, the body has transitioned into the post-absorptive state, where the liver maintains blood glucose levels primarily through the breakdown of stored glycogen. While gluconeogenesis also contributes, glycogenolysis is the immediate and primary mechanism for glucose release during this time interval. Skeletal muscle stores cannot contribute directly to blood glucose because they lack glucose-6-phosphatase.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
