    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The cell membrane is freely permeable to ions X, Y, and Z, and the membrane potential will be a weighted average of their equilibrium potentials based on permeability. Since the equilibrium potentials for X (-30 mV) and Y (-40 mV) are known, and no value is given for Z, blocking Z's permeability will shift the membrane potential closer to the weighted average of only X and Y. If Z had a more negative equilibrium potential than the current membrane potential, blocking it would cause depolarization; if less negative, it could cause hyperpolarization — but without Z’s equilibrium potential, the key is that any change in permeability to an ion will alter the potential.\n\nHowever, because the membrane was permeable to all three and now one is blocked, a change is expected. Given typical assumptions and ion behavior, blocking an ion (especially if it's potassium-like with high resting permeability) often leads to depolarization.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
