    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks which statement about inverted counterpoint is incorrect. Inverted counterpoint involves flipping intervals around a chosen axis pitch. The key is that the interval qualities (major/minor) and tonal implications change differently depending on the axis. Option B claims that using the IIº as axis leaves horizontal melody intervals unchanged, but inversion around any axis changes interval directions and can alter qualities (e.g., a major third ascending becomes a minor sixth descending). In fact, intervals are not preserved in quality; only the distance in semitones is preserved if using strict chromatic inversion, but diatonic inversion changes qualities. Option B is likely incorrect because the quality of intervals in melodic motion does change (e.g., a major second may become a minor seventh, etc.). Thus, B is the incorrect statement.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
