e question. But assuming Option D is intended to be the one with E, G, B, C — but it says G♭.\n\nPerhaps B in the option is B natural, which is 6th in D and not in F (F has D as 6th). So no.\n\nAfter reevaluating, the only note that is unstable in both keys is E — as the 7th of F major and not in D major's unstable (D's 7th is C♯). E is the 2nd in D? No, 2nd is E — yes! In D major, scale: D E F♯ G A B C♯ — so E is 2nd, unstable. In F major: F G A B♭ C D E — E is 7th, unstable. So E is unstable in both. G: in D is 4th, unstable; in F is 2nd, unstable — so G is also unstable in both. So E and G are unstable in both D and F major.\n\nSo we need E and G. Looking at options: Option B has E and G (natural), so G, not G♭. Option B: E, G, B♯, C. So E and G are present. B♯ is not in either key — D has B natural (6th), F has B♭, so B♯ is wrong. But G is natural, which is correct.\n\nBut Option A and D have G♭, which is G-flat, not G-natural.\n\nSo only Option B has G (natural). Therefore, despite B♯ being incorrect, it is the only option containing both E and G (natural), which are the common unstable degrees.\n\nBut C is also there — C is not unstable in a way common to both — in D, C♯ is 7th, but C natural is not in D major. In F, C is 5th, stable. So C is stable in F, not unstable.\n\nSo the option includes C, which is stable in F, so shouldn't be included as \"unstable\".\n\nBut the question is \"contains the unstable scale degrees in both\" — meaning the set that includes the unstable ones of both keys? Or the common ones?\n\nIf it's the common ones, then only E and G. But no option has only E and G.\n\nIf it's the full list for both, then we need E, G (from D), and G, B♭, D, E (from F), plus B, C♯ from D.\n\nStill, no option matches.\n\nGiven the options, and that Option B has E and G (natural), and G is correct for both, while other options have G♭, which is wrong, so Option B is the only one with G natural.\n\nMoreover, perhaps B♯ is a typo for B natural or B♭.\n\nGiv