      "generated_answer": "The concessions described in the Treaty of Nanjing established specific ports where British merchants could reside and conduct trade under the supervision of consular officers, which historically evolved into British spheres of influence in China. These treaty ports functioned as areas where British economic and political pressure dominated, characterized by extraterritoriality and focused trade interests rather than formal colonial governance or independent businesses.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
