    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4605",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"At least one of the [world's] societies would have to somehow enormously increase its productivity [in order to achieve global hegemony]. That quantum jump would have to be made before the various scientific, technological, agricultural, and industrial revolutions on which our post-quantum-leap world rests. It could only be accomplished by exploiting the ecosystems, mineral resources, and human assets of whole continents outside the lands of the society making the jump. Western Europe did just that by means of its brutality and guns and, more important, by geographical and ecological luck.\"\nCopyright © 2015 Cambridge University Press.\nAlfred Crosby, historian, Ecological Imperialism, 2004\nWhich of the following would best support the author's assertion regarding the \"quantum jump\" that would help Western Europe achieve global hegemony between 1450 and 1750 C.E.?\n\nOptions: \nA. The discovery and colonization of Australia\nB. The cultural exchange with Native American tribes\nC. The development of a centralized government in Europe\nD. The reformation of Catholic Christianity\nE. The conquest of the Aztec Empire\nF. The industrial revolution in Britain\nG. The isolationism of Tokugawa Japan\nH. The colonization of the interior of Africa\nI. The construction of the Great Wall of China\nJ. The establishment of trade routes with China\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{E}.\n\nThe author argues that the \"quantum jump\" in productivity was achieved by exploiting \"human assets\" and \"ecosystems\" of continents outside Europe, specifically using \"brutality and guns.\" Among the options, the conquest of the Aztec Empire by Spain (1519–1521) provides the most direct historical example of utilizing military force to gain control over external resources and labor to bolster European power. Other options either predate the expansion period, represent different regions, or do not demonstrate the \"brutal\" exploitation of colonial resources required to achieve the shift described.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 348,
        "output_tokens": 127,
        "total_tokens": 475
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00027749999999999997,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
