ilable_plain -= 1\n            elif available_logo > 0:\n                available_logo -= 1\n            else:\n                # Need to buy a logo T-shirt (since plain are exhausted)\n                bought_logo += 1\n            i += 1\n    \n    # However, the above simulation is missing a key point: \n    # We need to account for the fact that when we encounter a '0', we wash ALL worn T-shirts,\n    # so we regain all the T-shirts used in the previous consecutive non-zero days.\n    \n    # Let's revise: we need to group consecutive non-zero days and handle each group separately\n    # Because washing happens only on '0' days, and after washing, all T-shirts become available again\n    \n    available_plain = M\n    available_logo = 0\n    bought_logo = 0\n    i = 0\n    \n    while i < N:\n        if S[i] == '0':\n            # Wash all T-shirts - reset available T-shirts to original state\n            # (we don't lose any T-shirts, they're all washed and reusable)\n            i += 1\n            continue\n            \n        # Find the consecutive segment of non-zero days\n        j = i\n        while j < N and S[j] != '0':\n            j += 1\n            \n        # Now we have a segment from i to j-1 of non-zero days\n        segment = S[i:j]\n        \n        # Count how many '2's (mandatory logo) and '1's (optional) in this segment\n        mandatory_logo = segment.count('2')\n        optional_meals = segment.count('1')\n        \n        # We need at least `mandatory_logo` logo T-shirts for the mandatory days\n        # We can use plain T-shirts for the optional meals, as long as we have them\n        \n        # How many logo T-shirts do we need in this segment?\n        # The plain T-shirts can cover up to `available_plain` of the optional meals\n        # The remaining optional meals need logo T-shirts\n        remaining_optional = max(0, optional_meals - available_plain)\n        \n        # Total logo T-shirts needed for this segment = mandatory_logo + remaining_optional\n        total_logo_needed = mandatory_logo + remaining_optional\n        \n        # If we don't have enough logo T-shirts, we need to buy more\n        if total_logo_needed > available_logo:\n            bought_logo += total_logo_needed - available_logo\n            available_logo = total_logo_needed  # After buying, we have enough\n        \n        # After this segment, we don't wash until we hit a '0', but since we're moving to next group,\n        # and we're already processing consecutive segments separated by '0's, we can reset our perspective\n        # But note: after this segment, the T-shirts used are dirty and won't be available until the next '0'\n        # So for the next segment, we start with available_plain (unchanged, since we only used them, they're dirty)\n        # and available_logo (we used them, they're dirty, so not available until next '0')\n        \n        # However, we must note: in this segment, we used:\n        # - `min(available_plain, optional_meals)` plain T-shirts (they become dirty)\n        # - `mandatory_logo + max(0, optional_meals - available_plain)` logo T-shirts (dirty)\n        #\n        # But since the next segment will start after a '0', we know that when we hit a '0' we'll wash all\n        # So we reset available_plain and available_logo after each '0' segment\n        # Actually, we don't need to track dirty T-shirts because the washing reclaims them,\n        # so our available_plain and available_logo should be reset to original state after each '0'\n        #\n        # But we haven't encountered the '0' yet in this code - we're grouping by segments\n        # So after finishing a segment, we reset our available T-shirts to the original state?\n        # Actually NO - because the T-shirts we used are dirty and not available until washing\n        # The washing happens at '0' days, so the only way we regain T-shirts is after encountering a '0'\n        # So after we finish this segment, we are still holding the dirty T-shirts, they are not available for next segment unless there's a '0'\n        #\n        # But we have already handled the '0' separation - so next segment starts only after a '0', meaning we have washed everything\n        #\n        # Actually, the key insight is: after each segment of non-zero days, we will eventually encounter a '0' (or the end)\n        # But the washing only happens when we hit a '0'. So if the next segment is after a '0', we reset.\n        # But in our grouping, we're breaking at '0's, and the next segment starts after a '0', meaning that at the boundary,\n        # the washing has occurred and we have all T-shirts back.\n        #\n        # Therefore, we can assume that after each segment (before we process the next one), we have ALL T-shirts available again\n        # So reset available_plain and available_logo to their original values? NO!\n        #\n        # That's not correct. We are using T-shirts, and they become dirty. The washing recovers them after a '0'.\n        # Since we've processed a segment and the next segment starts after a '0', we actually do get the T-shirts back!\n        #\n        # So after we finish a segment (and before the next segment if it exists), we know there was a '0' in between\n        # Therefore, we should reset available_plain and available_logo to their original state for the next segment\n        #\n        # BUT: we don't need to! Because the problem is that we're processing the segments in sequence\n        # and between segments there's a '0' day, so we get back ALL the T-shirts we used in the previous segment.\n        # Therefore, for each segment we start with the original M plain T-shirts and whatever logo T-shirts we've accumulated,\n        # but we haven't washed yet! However, we have already bought the logo T-shirts we need and they are washed and available.\n        #\n        # Actually, the correct interpretation: we start each segment with:\n      