den pairs have comp[x]=a and comp[y]=b (or vice versa).\n- Then for query (p,q) with components a=comp[p], b=comp[q]:\n   - If a == b: answer \"Yes\"\n   - Else: if count[(min(a,b), max(a,b))] > 0 → then adding this edge would connect that pair → \"No\", else \"Yes\"\n\nHowever: K and number of components can be up to 2e5. The number of distinct (a,b) pairs with forbidden connections is at most K, but we cannot store a full matrix.\n\nAlternative: Instead of storing counts for all component pairs, we can store for each pair (a,b) only whether there is at least one forbidden pair spanning them.\n\nBut how to answer quickly? We can use a hashset of tuples (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2)) for all forbidden pairs.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Build union-find (or BFS/DFS) but note: N, M up to 2e5 → use DSU (union-find) for efficiency.\n\nHowever: the graph may have self-loops and multi-edges. DSU can ignore self-loops (they don’t change connectivity) and multi-edges are harmless.\n\nSo:\n- Use DSU to compute components of the original graph.\n\n2. Compress component ids: after DSU, for each vertex i, we get root = dsu.find(i). Then we can map each root to a compressed id (0-indexed or just keep as root, but better compress to small integers).\n\nLet comp[i] = compressed component id of vertex i.\n\n3. Build a set `forbidden_pairs_set` of tuples (min(comp[x_i], comp[y_i]), max(comp[x_i], comp[y_i])) for each forbidden pair i.\n\n4. For each query (p, q):\n   - a = comp[p], b = comp[q]\n   - if a == b: print \"Yes\"\n   - else: if (min(a,b), max(a,b)) in `forbidden_pairs_set`: print \"No\"\n        else: print \"Yes\"\n\nWhy is this correct?\n- Because adding edge (p,q) merges components a and b.\n- If there is any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) that lies in components a and b respectively, then after merging, x_i and y_i become connected → graph is no longer good → answer \"No\".\n- Otherwise, no forbidden pair becomes connected → still good.\n\nNote: Since the original graph is good, all forbidden pairs are across different components, so each forbidden pair contributes exactly one unordered pair (a,b) with a≠b.\n\nAlso note: multiple forbidden pairs might contribute the same (a,b) pair, but we only need to know if at least one exists.\n\nLet’s validate with sample:\n\nInput:\n6 6\n1 2\n2 3\n2 3\n3 1\n5 4\n5 5   <-- self-loop (ignore)\nK=3\n1 5\n2 6\n4 3\nQ=4\n2 5\n2 6\n5 6\n5 4\n\nFirst, build graph:\nEdges: (1,2), (2,3), (2,3) [multi], (3,1), (5,4), (5,5) [self-loop]\n\nComponents:\n- Vertices 1,2,3: connected (via 1-2-3-1)\n- Vertex 4 and 5: connected (edge 5-4)\n- Vertex 6: isolated.\n\nSo components:\n- C1: {1,2,3} → comp[1]=comp[2]=comp[3]=0 (say)\n- C2: {4,5} → comp[4]=comp[5]=1\n- C3: {6} → comp[6]=2\n\nForbidden pairs:\n(1,5): comp[1]=0, comp[5]=1 → pair (0,1)\n(2,6): comp[2]=0, comp[6]=2 → pair (0,2)\n(4,3): comp[4]=1, comp[3]=0 → pair (0,1) [same as first]\n\nSo forbidden_pairs_set = {(0,1), (0,2)}\n\nQueries:\n1. (2,5): comp[2]=0, comp[5]=1 → (0,1) ∈ set → \"No\"\n2. (2,6): comp[2]=0, comp[6]=2 → (0,2) ∈ set → \"No\"\n3. (5,6): comp[5]=1, comp[6]=2 → (1,2) not in set → \"Yes\"\n4. (5,4): comp[5]=1, comp[4]=1 → same component → \"Yes\"\n\nMatches sample output.\n\nImplementation details:\n- DSU implementation with path compression and union by rank/size for efficiency.\n- Note: self-loops (u==v) do nothing — we can skip union for them, but DSU union with same component is harmless (but avoid extra work).\n- Multi-edges: same component → ignore.\n\nSteps in code:\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Initialize DSU for N+1 (1-indexed).\n3. For each edge (u, v):\n   - if u != v: union(u, v)\n4. Compress component ids:\n   - Create a list of roots for vertices 1..N: roots = [dsu.find(i) for i in 1..N]\n   - Map each distinct root to a compressed id (e.g., using dictionary: root_to_id)\n   - comp[i] = root_to_id[dsu.find(i)]\n5. Read K, then for each forbidden pair (x, y):\n   - a = comp[x], b = comp[y]\n   - add tuple (min(a,b), max(a,b)) to a set `forbidden_pairs`