      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem efficiently, we first sort the array `nums` in non-decreasing order. By sorting, for any subset of heroes, the maximum element will always be the one with the largest index in our sorted array, and the minimum element will depend on the subset chosen.\n\nFor a fixed hero `nums[i]` acting as the maximum element, we want to calculate the sum of `nums[i]^2 * min(group)` for all subsets where `nums[i]` is the maximum. If `nums[i]` is the maximum, then all other elements in the subset must be chosen from indices `0` to `i-1`. Using prefix sums of the contributions of the minimums leads to an $O(n \\log n)$ solution.\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # dp[i] will store the sum of (min(group) * nums[i]^2) for all groups\n    # where nums[i] is the maximum element.\n    # Specifically, if we consider nums[i] as the maximum, the groups are\n    # {nums[i]}, {nums[i], ...}, etc.\n    # The sum for a fixed i is:\n    # nums[i]^2 * (nums[i] + sum_{j=0 to i-1} (min_contribution_ending_at_j))\n    \n    # Let f[i] be the sum of minimums of all subsets of nums[0...i] \n    # that include nums[i] as the minimum.\n    # Through derivation, the factor for nums[i] is obtained by:\n    # current_sum = nums[i] + 2 * sum_{j=0 to i-1} (contribution_of_j)\n    \n    ans = 0\n    dp = [0] * n\n    prefix_sum = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # dp[i] is the sum of minimums of all subsets ending at i\n        # where nums[i] is the minimum.\n        # This is nums[i] + sum(dp[0...i-1])\n        dp[i] = (nums[i] + prefix_sum[i]) % MOD\n        prefix_sum[i+1] = (prefix_sum[i] + dp[i]) % MOD\n        \n        # Power contribution with nums[i] as the maximum\n        contribution = (nums[i] * nums[i]) * dp[i]\n        ans = (ans + contribution) % MOD\n        \n    return ans\n```",
