      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfWays(n: int, x: int) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Generate all possible base values whose x-th power is <= n\n    # The maximum base value is the largest integer k such that k^x <= n\n    max_base = 1\n    while (max_base + 1) ** x <= n:\n        max_base += 1\n    \n    # dp[i] will represent the number of ways to make sum i\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # Base case: one way to make sum 0 (using no numbers)\n    \n    # For each base value from 1 to max_base\n    for base in range(1, max_base + 1):\n        power_val = base ** x\n        # Update dp in reverse order to ensure each number is used at most once\n        for s in range(n, power_val - 1, -1):\n            dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - power_val]) % MOD\n    \n    return dp[n]\n```",
