use a potion we haven’t picked up.**\n\n   So here's the key realization from known similar problems (e.g., ABC295F):\n\n   > We can simulate **forward**, and for every monster, we **require** that a potion of that type must have been picked up before. So we can **delay potion pickup** until we **absolutely need it**.\n\n   But how do we minimize the **maximum inventory**?\n\n   **Optimal strategy:**\n   - For each potion type, **record the positions of potion finds and monster encounters**.\n   - For each monster of type `x`, we must have a potion `x` picked up **before** it.\n   - We can postpone picking up potions until the moment we need them.\n   - But if we postpone, we might not have picked it up yet when the monster comes.\n\n   So here is the correct strategy:\n\n   ### ✅ Correct Algorithm:\n\n   We will simulate the events and use a **greedy \"reserve\"** approach:\n\n   1. For **each potion type**, we will **precompute**:\n      - All potion generation events (t_i=1, x_i)\n      - All monster encounter events (t_i=2, x_i)\n\n   2. For **each monster** of type `x`, we check whether we can assign a potion from the **available potion events before this monster**.\n\n   3. But we want to **minimize the maximum number of potions held at any time**. To do that, we use a known greedy:\n\n   > **Use a \"deferral\" strategy**: For each potion, do not pick it up immediately. Instead, wait until we need a potion of that type. When a monster of type `x` appears and we don’t have any picked-up potion, we must pick up one of the **latest available** potion of type `x` (to preserve earlier ones for future monsters) — but we cannot go back.\n\n   So perhaps we need to simulate **backward** to compute the minimum required potions.\n\n   Actually, we can solve this using a **greedy with a priority queue**.\n\n   ### ✅ Final Known Approach (from similar AtCoder problems):\n\n   We can do:\n\n   - For each potion type `x`, keep a list of potion event indices (positions).\n  