      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    events = []\n    idx = 1\n    for _ in range(N):\n        t = int(input_data[idx])\n        x = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        events.append((t, x))\n        idx += 2\n\n    # Find the indices of monster encounters for each potion type\n    monster_indices = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    for i in range(N - 1, -1, -1):\n        t, x = events[i]\n        if t == 2:\n            monster_indices[x].append(i)\n\n    # To determine if he can win, we need to pick up a potion if it's needed\n    # for a future monster. Specifically, for a type x, we must pick up \n    # potions corresponding to the monsters encountered.\n    # To minimize K, we greedily discard the oldest potion if we're at capacity\n    # but we only know the capacity limit after binary search.\n    # Actually, we can simulate to see if he survives at all first.\n    \n    # Pre-process: For every monster at index 'i' of type 'x',\n    # we MUST have picked up some potion of type 'x' before 'i'.\n    # To minimize K, use a priority queue to check capacity.\n    \n    ans_actions = []\n    potion_indices = []\n    type_ptr = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(N):\n        if events[i][0] == 1:\n            potion_indices.append(i)\n    \n    # Check feasibility:\n    needed_potions = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    for i in range(N):\n        t, x = events[i]\n        if t == 2:\n            if not monster_indices[x]:\n                print(\"-1\")\n                return\n            needed_potions[x].append(monster_indices[x].pop())\n            \n    # Simulation with binary search for K_min\n    def check(K):\n        actions = {} # index: 0 or 1\n        current_potions = [] # list of (next_use_index, potion_type)\n        inventory = {} # type: count\n        \n        # Potion indices for each type\n        type_to_indices = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n        for i, idx in enumerate(potion_indices):\n            type_to_indices[events[idx][1]].append(i)\n        \n        # ptr for which potion we are at for each type\n        ptr = [0] * (N + 1)\n        \n        # Simulation\n        heap = [] # (next_use_index, potion_type)\n        cur_k = 0\n        \n        for i in range(N):\n            t, x = events[i]\n            if t == 1:\n                # Decide to pick up: Only if type x has a future monster\n                if ptr[x] < len(needed_potions[x]):\n                    actions[i] = 1\n                    next_use = needed_potions[x][ptr[x]]\n                    ptr[x] += 1\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (next_use, x))\n                    cur_k += 1\n                    if cur_k > K:\n                        # Must discard one\n                        discard_idx, discard_type = heapq.heappop(heap)\n                        cur_k -= 1\n                        # Find the action associated with this and set to 0\n                        # This is tricky because we need to map back to original input idx\n                        # Actually, just search for the latest action of type discard_type\n                        # Optimization: store the action index in the heap\n                        return False, None\n                else:\n                    actions[i] = 0\n            else:\n                # Monster\n                if cur_k > 0: # This is a simplification; verification needed\n                    pass\n        return True, actions\n\n    # Since the problem allows any valid sequence for K_min, \n    # we can use a max-heap of event indices to limit capacity.\n    # The actual strategy is: always pick up potion if it's needed for a monster.\n    # If the held potions count exceeds K, drop the one whose next use is furthest.\n\n    # Correct greedy approach:\n    # 1. Identify which potions are needed. For each monster of type X, \n    #    pair it with the *latest* available potion of type X before it.\n    actual_picks = [0] * len(potion_indices)\n    potion_slots = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    for i, idx in enumerate(potion_indices):\n        potion_slots[events[idx][1]].append(i)\n    \n    monster_needs = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        if events[i][0] == 2:\n            monster_needs.append((i, events[i][1]))\n            \n    # For every monster, greedily take the nearest preceding potion\n    # Actually, iterate backwards to match monster to latest possible potion\n    mapping = {} # monster_idx -> potion_list_idx\n    needed = []\n    for i in range(N-1, -1, -1):\n        if events[i][0] == 2:\n            needed.append(i)\n        elif events[i][0] == 1 and needed:\n            x = events[i][1]\n            if x == events[needed[-1]][1]:\n                m_idx = needed.pop()\n                actual_picks[potion_indices.index(i)] = 1\n                mapping[m_idx] = i\n    \n    if needed:\n        print(\"-1\")\n        return\n\n    # Calculate K\n    K = 0\n    cur = 0\n    max_k = 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        if events[i][0] == 1 and actual_picks[potion_indices.index(i)]:\n            cur += 1\n        elif events[i][0] == 2:\n            cur -= 1\n        max_k = max(max_k, cur)\n    \n    print(max_k)\n    print(*(actual_picks))\n\nsolve()\n```",
