      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Count frequency of each number\n    from collections import Counter\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    \n    # dp[s] represents the number of ways to achieve sum s\n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # Empty multiset has sum 0\n    \n    # Process each unique number\n    for num, count in freq.items():\n        if num == 0:\n            # Zeros: each zero can be included 0 to count times, each contributing 0 to sum\n            # So we multiply all dp values by (count + 1)\n            for i in range(r + 1):\n                dp[i] = (dp[i] * (count + 1)) % MOD\n            continue\n        \n        # For non-zero numbers, use optimized counting\n        # We'll use a temporary array to avoid interference during updates\n        new_dp = dp[:]  # Copy current state\n        \n        # For each possible remainder modulo num (to apply sliding window technique)\n        for rem in range(num):\n            # Use sliding window to efficiently compute the sum of dp values in window\n            window_sum = 0\n            # Process indices that are congruent to rem modulo num, in increasing order\n            for s in range(rem, r + 1, num):\n                window_sum = (window_sum + dp[s]) % MOD\n                # If the window contains more than count+1 elements, subtract the element leaving the window\n                if s - num * (count + 1) >= 0:\n                    window_sum = (window_sum - dp[s - num * (count + 1)] + MOD) % MOD\n                # Add the window_sum contribution to new_dp[s]\n                new_dp[s] = (new_dp[s] + window_sum) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Sum all dp values in range [l, r]\n    result = 0\n    for i in range(l, r + 1):\n        result = (result + dp[i]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
