      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    \n    # dp[s] will store the number of ways to get sum s\n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    # Handle zeros separately: any subset of zeros can be added to any valid non-zero subset\n    zero_count = counts.pop(0, 0)\n    \n    # Process each distinct number x with count c\n    # This is a variation of the bounded knapsack problem\n    for x, c in counts.items():\n        if x == 0: continue\n        \n        # We use a sliding window sum to optimize the transition\n        # dp_new[s] = sum(dp[s - k*x]) for 0 <= k <= c\n        # dp_new[s] = dp_new[s-x] + dp[s] - dp[s - (c+1)*x]\n        new_dp = list(dp)\n        for s in range(x, r + 1):\n            new_dp[s] = (new_dp[s-x] + dp[s]) % MOD\n            if s >= (c + 1) * x:\n                new_dp[s] = (new_dp[s] - dp[s - (c + 1) * x] + MOD) % MOD\n        dp = new_dp\n        \n    # Each subset of non-zero elements can be combined with any subset of zeros.\n    # Number of subsets of zero_count zeros is 2^zero_count.\n    # Actually, we can pick any number of zeros from 0 to zero_count, so total (zero_count + 1) ways\n    ans = 0\n    for s in range(l, r + 1):\n        ans = (ans + dp[s]) % MOD\n    \n    return (ans * (zero_count + 1)) % MOD\n```",
