      "generated_answer": ", I need to find the minimal window width W such that the sentence can be fit in ≤ M lines.\n\nThis is a classic \"text justification\" / \"line wrapping\" optimization problem, where we want to minimize the maximum line width (here, the window width), given a constraint on the number of lines.\n\nApproach:\n- We can use binary search on W (window width).\n- For a given W, check if it's possible to fit all N words into ≤ M lines.\n- The check function (can_fit(W)) will use a greedy algorithm: place as many words as possible on each line without exceeding W.\n\nHow to compute line width for a group of words?\n- For words with indices i..j (0-based), the line width = sum(L[i..j]) + (j - i)  [because there are (j-i) spaces between j-i+1 words]\n- So width = (L[i] + L[i]+1 + ... + L[j]) + (j - i)\n\nConstraints for a valid line:\n- Each individual word must fit: L[i] ≤ W (otherwise impossible)\n- The line width as above must be ≤ W\n\nBinary search bounds:\n- Lower bound: max(L_i) (since each word must fit alone)\n- Upper bound: sum(L_i) + (N-1) (all words on one line)\n\nSince N can be up to 2×10^5 and L_i up to 10^9, the sum can be up to ~2×10^14, so we use appropriate bounds.\n\nSteps:\n1. Read N, M and the list of L_i\n2. Define can_fit(W) function:\n   - If any L_i > W, return False\n   - Count lines needed:\n     * Start with line_count = 1, current_line_width = 0\n     * For each word width w in L:\n       - If current_line_width == 0: \n           - can always add first word on a new line (since w ≤ W by check above)\n           - current_line_width = w\n       - Else: \n           - needed_width = current_line_width + 1 + w  (1 for space, w for word)\n           - If needed_width ≤ W: add word to current line\n           - Else: start new line (line_count += 1), current_line_width = w\n   - Return line_count ≤ M\n3. Binary search for minimal W where can_fit(W) is True\n4. Print the result\n\nLet me code this carefully.\n\nNote: Sample