ible numbers:\n\n- For each bit position `b`, we have `bit_count[b]` bits to distribute.\n- To maximize the sum of squares, we want to assign each bit to the **largest numbers possible**.\n\nSo the optimal way is:\n\n> Assign the bits (from each bit position) to the **first `bit_count[b]` numbers**, starting from index 0 to n-1, where the first number gets as many bits as possible, then the second, etc.\n\nIn other words, construct `n` numbers as follows:\n\n- `result[i]` = sum over all bit positions `b` where the `i`-th largest number gets that bit.\n\nBut how do we assign bits optimally?\n\nWe can do:\n\n- For each bit position `b`, we have `bit_count[b]` ones. We assign each of these ones to the **first `bit_count[b]` numbers** (i.e., the numbers we are going to choose for maximum sum of squares).\n\nThen, each number `i` (for `i` from 0 to `n-1`) will have value:\n\n```\nvalue[i] = sum_{b=0}^{31} (1 << b) * I[ i < bit_count[b] ]\n```\n\nWait — this is backwards!\n\nActually:\n\nWe have `bit_count[b]` bits of type `b`. To make the largest possible numbers, we should give **each bit to the `bit_count[b]` largest numbers**.\n\nBut since we are building numbers from bits, we can do:\n\nConstruct an array of `n` numbers, initially 0.\n\nFor each bit position `b`:\n\n- We have `bit_count[b]` bits to distribute.\n- To maximize the sum of squares of the top `k` numbers, it's best to put each of these `bit_count[b]` bits into `bit_count[b]` **distinct** numbers, and specifically into the numbers with the **highest current values** — to make them even bigger.\n\nThe optimal strategy is:\n\n1. Count the total number of ones in each bit position across all numbers.\n2. For `i = 0` to `n-1`, construct `nums[i]` by setting bit `b` if the `i`-th largest \"bucket\" (in terms of bit assignment priority) gets that bit.\n\nBut here’s the elegant insight:\n\n> The best way to assign bits so that the **sum of squares of the top k numbers is maximized** is to form numbers greedily:\n\n> For each bit `b`, assign it to the **top `bit_count[b]` numbers** (i.e., the numbers with the highest indices in the sorted-by-value order).\n\nBut we are building the numbers, and we want to maximize the sum of squares of the `k` largest.\n\nThe optimal construction is:\n\n- Create an array `res` of length `n` (or just use a count per number) — for each number, we will compute its value.\n- For bit `b`, we have `c = bit_count[b]` bits to assign.\n- We assign one bit of type `b` to the first `c` numbers in the list (from index 0 to c-1). Why?\n\nActually, we want the **largest numbers to get as many high-value bits as possible**. So the best way is:\n\n- Start with `n` zeros.\n- For **each bit position from high to low** (but order doesn't matter since bits are independent), we have `c` ones.\n- Assign those `c` ones to the first `c` numbers (i.e., the `c` numbers that will be the largest).\n\nWait — let me clarify with an example.\n\nSuppose we have two bits: bit 0 (value 1) and bit 1 (value 2). Total counts: bit0: 2, bit1: 1.\n\nWe have 3 numbers.\n\nWe want to assign bits to numbers to maximize sum of squares of top k=2.\n\nAssign bit1 (value 2) to the first number → number0 = 2\n\nAssign bit0 (value 1) to first two numbers → number0 +=1 → 3, number1 = 1\n\nSo numbers = [3, 1, 0]\n\nSum of squares of top 2: 9 + 1 = 10.\n\nAlternative: assign bit1 to number0, bit0 to number1 and number2 → [2,1,1] → top 2: 4 + 1 = 5 → worse.\n\nAnother: assign bit1 to number1, bit0 to number0 and number1 → [1,3,0] → same as first case.\n\nSo optimal is: **each bit is assigned to the first `bit_count[b]` numbers** (i.e., numbers 0 to `bit_count[b]-1`).\n\nWhy? Because we are building the numbers, and we can sort them arbitrarily (since operations allow rearrangement in effect), we can choose to make the top `bit_count[b]` numbers have this bit. And if we do this for all bits, the numbers will be sorted in descending order: number0 has all bits that appear at least once, number1 has all bits that appear at least twice, etc.\n\nThus, the best strategy:\n\n### Algorithm:\n\n1. Count the number of 1s in each bit position (0 to 30) across all `nums`. Let `freq[b]` = number of elements with bit `b` set.\n\n2. We'll construct `n` numbers, where for `i = 0` to `n-1`, the value of the `i`-th number is:\n   ```\n   num[i] = sum_{b=0}^{30} (1 << b) if i < freq[b]\n   ```\n   Why? Because we can assign the `freq[b]` ones of bit `b` to the first `freq[b]` numbers (index 0 to `freq[b]-1`). Since we want to maximize the sum of squares of the top `k`, and `num[0] >= num[1] >= ...`, we take the first `k` numbers.\n\n3. Then, compute `sum_{i=0}^{k-1} num[i]^2 mod (10^9+7)`.\n\nThis works because:\n- The total number of 1-bits is preserved.\n- The greedy assignment maximizes the variance → maximizes the sum of squares.\n- Larger numbers get more bits, so they contribute more to the sum of squares.\n\n### Example walkthrough: nums = [2,6,5,8], k = 2\n\nBinary:\n\n- 2: 0010\n- 6: 0110\n- 5: 0101\n- 8: 1000\n\nBit counts:\n\n- bit0 (1): appears in 5 → count=1\n- bit1 (2): appears in 2,6 → count=2\n- bit2 (4): appears in 5,6 → count=2\n- bit3 (8): appears in 8 → count=1\n\nfreq = [1, 2, 2,",
