      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Count the number of 1s at each bit position across all numbers\n    bit_count = [0] * 32  # Since nums[i] <= 10^9, 31 bits are enough, but use 32 for safety\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        for i in range(32):\n            if num & (1 << i):\n                bit_count[i] += 1\n    \n    # To maximize sum of squares, we want to maximize individual elements\n    # Note: The operation allows us to rearrange bits among numbers\n    # Specifically, for each bit position, the total count of 1s is preserved\n    # We can concentrate bits into fewer numbers to maximize the sum of squares\n    \n    # The optimal strategy is to create numbers that are as large as possible\n    # For each number we construct, we set bit i to 1 if there is at least one 1 remaining at position i\n    # But since we want to maximize sum of squares, we should concentrate bits into as few numbers as possible\n    # Greedy approach: create k numbers by distributing bits optimally\n    \n    # Actually, the sum of squares is maximized when the numbers are as \"uneven\" as possible\n    # We can form numbers by greedily setting bits where possible\n    \n    # For each bit position i, we have bit_count[i] ones available\n    # In optimal configuration, we can have at most bit_count[i] numbers with bit i set\n    # To maximize sum of squares, we want to maximize the largest numbers\n    # So we create numbers by setting bits greedily from highest bit to lowest\n    \n    # Create an array of k zeros\n    arr = [0] * k\n    \n    # For each bit position from highest to lowest\n    for i in range(31, -1, -1):\n        # We have bit_count[i] ones to place at this bit position\n        # To maximize sum of squares, place them in the largest numbers possible\n        # Since the numbers are being built up, we want to set this bit in the first bit_count[i] numbers\n        # (which are the largest so far due to higher bits already set)\n        for j in range(bit_count[i]):\n            arr[j] |= (1 << i)\n    \n    # Now take the first k elements (all elements) and compute sum of squares\n    result = 0\n    for num in arr:\n        result = (result + num * num) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
