    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To maximize the sum of squares after the given operation, we should observe that the operation `nums[i] = nums[i] & nums[j]` and `nums[j] = nums[i] | nums[j]` essentially moves the set bits from one number to another. Specifically, for any bit position $b$, if at least one number in the array has the $b$-th bit set, we can eventually move that bit to be part of the largest available number.\n\nBy performing this operation repeatedly, we can concentrate bits into as many numbers as possible. In the end, we can obtain a set of numbers where each bit position $b$ contributes to the count of numbers that have the $b$-th bit set. To maximize the sum of squares, we should greedily assign these bits to the numbers to make them as large as possible.\n\nHere is the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    # There are at most 30 bits since nums[i] <= 10^9\n    bit_counts = [0] * 32\n    for x in nums:\n        for i in range(32):\n            if (x >> i) & 1:\n                bit_counts[i] += 1\n    \n    # We want to greedily pick the k largest numbers.\n    # We can reconstruct k numbers such that the j-th number \n    # has the i-th bit set if bit_counts[i] > 0.\n    # To maximize sum of squares, we fill the largest numbers first.\n    \n    final_nums = [0] * len(nums)\n    for i in range(32):\n        for j in range(bit_counts[i]):\n            final_nums[j] |= (1 << i)\n            \n    # Sort to pick the largest k elements\n    final_nums.sort(reverse=True)\n    \n    ans = 0\n    for i in range(k):\n        ans = (ans + final_nums[i] * final_nums[i]) % MOD\n        \n    return ans\n```",
