      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, the key insight is that the operation:\n- `nums[i] = nums[i] AND nums[j]`\n- `nums[j] = nums[i] OR nums[j]`\n\nThis operation transfers bits from the AND result to the OR result without changing the total sum of the array. In fact, it moves bits from one number to another, potentially concentrating bits into fewer numbers.\n\nThe effect is that over many operations, we can concentrate all the bits from all numbers into the largest k numbers we want to pick for the square sum.\n\nSince squaring favors larger numbers (due to quadratic growth), the optimal strategy is:\n1. Count the total bits at each position across all numbers\n2. Assign those bits to the k largest numbers we'll pick\n3. The maximum sum of squares comes from making k numbers as large as possible\n\nHere's the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    bit_count = [0] * 32\n    \n    # Count bits at each position across all numbers\n    for num in nums:\n        for i in range(32):\n            if num & (1 << i):\n                bit_count[i] += 1\n    \n    # Build the k largest possible numbers\n    result = [0] * k\n    for i in range(31, -1, -1):\n        bits = bit_count[i]\n        # Assign this bit to the first 'bits' numbers (or all k if bits > k)\n        for j in range(min(bits, k)):\n            result[j] |= (1 << i)\n    \n    # Calculate sum of squares\n    total = 0\n    for val in result:\n        total = (total + (val * val) % MOD) % MOD\n    \n    return total\n```",
