ll ancestors a of v: is there an insurance on a with y >= (depth[v] - depth[a])?\n\nBut we have M insurances, and N nodes, so checking each node against all its ancestors would be O(N * depth) which in worst-case (chain) is O(N^2) = 9e10, too slow.\n\nWe need to flip it:\n\nProcess the insurances and for each insurance (x, y), we want to mark all v in the subtree of x with depth[v] <= depth[x] + y.\n\nWe can use DFS and store for each node the maximum \"coverage threshold\" that reaches it? Specifically, we can do:\n\nWe'll create an array `max_coverage` of length N+1, which for each node v, we want to store the maximum value of (depth[x] + y) from any insurance (x,y) such that x is an ancestor of v.\n\nThen node v is covered if `max_coverage[v] >= depth[v]`.\n\nWhy? Because if an insurance (x,y) covers v, then depth[v] <= depth[x] + y. So if we set `max_coverage[v] = max{ depth[x] + y }` over all ancestors x (including v) of v that have an insurance, then if `max_coverage[v] >= depth[v]`, v is covered.\n\nHow to compute `max_coverage[v]`?\n\nWe can do a DFS from root and propagate the maximum (depth[x] + y) value along the path.\n\nBut note: an insurance (x,y) should be considered for all descendants of x. So during DFS, when we visit a node x, we can push the value `depth[x] + y` (if there is an insurance on x) down the subtree.\n\nBut we have multiple insurances on the same x? We only care about the maximum (depth[x] + y) for that x? Yes, because if two insurances on the same x: (x, y1) and (x, y2), then we only need max(y1,y2) since it covers more.\n\nActually, we can aggregate per node: for each node x, let `best[x] = max{ y_i }` for all insurance events that have x_i = x. Then we want to propagate `depth[x] + best[x]` down the subtree.\n\nBut note: even if a node x has no insurance, but an ancestor of x has an insurance with a large y, we still get coverage.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Precompute depth of each node (using BFS from root).\n2. For each node, store a list of children.\n3. Create an array `ins` of size N+1: `ins[x] = max{ y_i such that x_i = x }`. If no insurance on x, then `ins[x] = -inf` (or 0, but we need to handle).\n   - Actually, if no insurance, then we can ignore. We'll initialize `ins` as 0 for all? But then if we do `depth[x] + ins[x]` and there's no insurance, that would be depth[x] (which is >= depth[v] for v=x? yes, but then v=x would be covered even if no insurance? No, we need to be careful.)\n\nActually, if there's an insurance on x with y_i, then it covers x and its descendants up to y_i generations. So the value we propagate is `depth[x] + y_i`. But if no insurance on x, then we don't get any contribution from x. However, we might get propagation from ancestors.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n- Create array `coverage_threshold` of length N+1, initially -10**18 (a very small number).\n- For each insurance (x_i, y_i), set:\n    `coverage_threshold[x_i] = max(coverage_threshold[x_i], depth[x_i] + y_i)`\n\n- Then do a DFS/BFS from the root and for each node, compute the maximum coverage threshold that has been passed down from any ancestor (including itself).\n\nWe can traverse the tree from root and for each node v:\n   `max_ancestor_threshold = max(parent's max_ancestor_threshold, coverage_threshold[v])`\n   Then if `max_ancestor_threshold >= depth[v]`, then v is covered.\n\nWait, let me test with sample 1:\n\nSample Input 1:\nN=7, M=3\nParents: p2 to p7: 1 2 1 3 3 3  → meaning:\np2=1, p3=2, p4=1, p5=3, p",
