e]` be the maximum remaining coverage at node.\n- For root: `budget[root] = insurance_budget[root]`\n- For child: `budget[child] = max(insurance_budget[child], budget[parent] - 1)`\n\nThen node is covered if `budget[node] >= 0`.\n\nLet me verify with the sample:\n- N=7, M=3\n- Parents: [1,2,1,3,3,3] (for persons 2-7)\n- Insurance: (1,1), (1,2), (4,3)\n\nBuild tree:\n- 1: children [2,4]\n- 2: child [3]\n- 3: children [5,6,7]\n- 4: no children\n\ninsurance_budget:\n- budget[1] = max(1,2) = 2\n- budget[4] = 3\n- others = 0\n\nDFS from 1:\n- Node 1: budget = 2 (≥0, covered)\n- Node 2: budget = max(0, 2-1) = 1 (≥0, covered)\n- Node 3: from 2, budget = max(0, 1-1) = 0 (≥0, covered)\n- Node 5: from 3, budget = max(0, 0-1) = 0 → max(0,-1) = 0, but insurance_budget[5]=0, so budget=0 (covered? yes since ≥0)\n- Wait, but insurance (1,1) only covers 1 generation, so person 3 should be covered by insurance (1,2) only.\n- Insurance (1,2) covers: person 1 (dist 0), persons 2,4 (dist 1), person 3 (dist 2)\n- So person 3 should be covered.\n\nWith my method:\n- Node 1: budget=2 ✓\n- Node 2: budget=max(0,2-1)=1 ✓\n- Node 3: budget=max(0,1-1)=0 ✓\n- Node 4: budget=max(3,2-1)=3 ✓\n- Node 5: budget=max(0,0-1)=0? But insurance (4,3) should cover person 4, but person 5 is a child of 3, not directly related to 4.\n\nWait, I think I made an error. Person 4 has no children in the sample, so insurance (4,3) only covers person 4.\n\nSo for node 5: parent is 3, budget from parent 3 is 0, so budget[5] = max(0, 0-1) = 0, but that's not right because no insurance covers person 5.\n\nLet me recalculate the insurance coverage manually:\n- Insurance (1,1): covers person 1, and 1st generation descendants: persons 2 and 4\n- Insurance (1,2): covers person 1, 1st gen (2,4), 2nd gen (3)\n- Insurance (4,3): covers person 4, and up to 3 generations descendants, but person 4 has no children, so only person 4.\n\nCovered people: 1,2,3,4 → 4 people.\n\nWith my budget method:\n- Node 1: budget=2 ✓\n- Node 2: b