ap is the minimum gap so far in the picked elements.\n    \n    # Since n <= 50 and k <= 50, and min_gap can be represented by differences between nums elements (only O(n^2) distinct values),\n    # we can use memoization and store min_gap as the actual value, but the state space is O(n * k * n * (n^2)) = O(n^3 * k) = 50^3 * 50 = 6.25e6 which is acceptable in Pyton with optimizations.\n    \n    # However, we can optimize min_gap by noting that it only depends on the differences, and we can use the fact that \n    # min_gap is always one of the nums[j] - nums[i] for j > i. So we can compress these values.\n    \n    # But let's try a simpler approach: iterate over all subsequences using DFS with pruning, but with n=50, 2^50 is too large.\n    \n    # Instead, use DP where we sort nums and use:\n    # dp[i][j] = list of (min_gap, count) for subsequences ending at i with length j\n    # But storing all min_gaps is expensive\n    \n    # Insight: We only need the sum of min_gaps, not the distribution.\n    # Let's define dp[i][j] = (total_sum_of_mins, count) for subsequences of length j ending at i\n    # But min_gap for a subsequence ending at i depends on all previous elements, not just the last one\n    \n    # Better approach: sort nums, then use DP with state (i, j, last_index) and compute the contribution incrementally.\n    # Actually, let's use the following approach:\n    \n    # After sorting, consider that for a subsequence with elements at indices i1 < i2 < ... < ik, \n    # its power = min(nums[i2]-nums[i1], nums[i3]-nums[i2], ..., nums[ik]-nums[i_{k-1}])\n    \n    # We can use linearity of summation: sum over all subsequences of length k of their power \n    # = sum over all gaps g (between consecutive elements in sorted array) of g * (number of subsequences \n    # where g is the minimum gap and occurs in the subsequence)\n    \n    # So let's fix a gap between indices a and b (a < b), with gap = nums[b] - nums[a].\n    # How many subsequences of length