      "generated_answer": "The antecedent of a conditional proposition is the \"if\" part—the condition that must be satisfied for the consequent to hold. In the statement “The university raises tuition **if, and only if**, both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it,” the phrase “both the governor approves of it and the board of trustees recommends it” constitutes the full antecedent (since “if and only if” means the antecedent and consequent are logically equivalent). None of the individual options (A, B, or C) alone represents the full antecedent, which is a conjunction of B and C.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
