    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To assess validity using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion $L ⊃ K$ is false only when $L$ is true and $K$ is false. With $L = T$, $K = F$, from the first premise $(K \\cdot L) ⊃ M$, since $K \\cdot L$ is false, the implication is true regardless of $M$; set $N = T$ to satisfy $N ∨ K$, then from $N ⊃ \\sim M$, $M$ must be false. Thus, $L = T$, $K = F$, $M = F$, $N = T$ makes all premises true and conclusion false — a counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
