    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_29",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nE ⊃ F\nG ⊃ ~F / ~G ∨ ~E\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when E and G are true and F is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when F and G are true and E is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We test validity by assuming all premises are true and the conclusion false, then check for consistency.  \nPremises: $E \\supset F$, $G \\supset \\sim F$; Conclusion: $\\sim G \\vee \\sim E$.  \nFor the conclusion to be false, both $\\sim G$ and $\\sim E$ must be false ⇒ $G$ and $E$ are true.  \nThen $E \\supset F$ true and $E$ true ⇒ $F$ must be true.  \nBut $G \\supset \\sim F$, $G$ true, and $F$ true ⇒ $\\sim F$ is false, so $G \\supset \\sim F$ is false.  \nThus, to make both premises true, we need $G \\supset \\sim F$ true; with $G$ true, this requires $F$ false.  \nSo try $E = \\text{true}$, $G = \\text{true}$, $F = \\text{false}$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = T ⊃ F = **false** → not acceptable (premise must be true).  \nTry $E = \\text{false}$, $G = \\text{true}$, $F = \\text{true}$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = F ⊃ T = **true**,  \n- $G \\supset \\sim F$ = T ⊃ F = **false** → invalid.  \nTry $E = \\text{true}$, $F = \\text{true}$, $G = \\text{true}$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = T ⊃ T = **true**,  \n- $G \\supset \\sim F$ = T ⊃ F = **false** → again, second premise false.  \nTry $E = \\text{true}$, $F = \\text{false}$, $G = \\text{false}$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = T ⊃ F = **false** → invalid.  \nTry $E = \\text{false}$, $F = \\text{false}$, $G = \\text{true}$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = F ⊃ F = **true**,  \n- $G \\supset \\sim F$ = T ⊃ T = **true**,  \n- Conclusion $\\sim G \\vee \\sim E$ = F ∨ T = **true** ⇒ argument holds.  \nTo find a counterexample, need premises true and conclusion false.  \nConclusion false ⇒ $G = T$, $E = T$. Then $E \\supset F$ true ⇒ $F = T$; $G \\supset \\sim F$ ⇒ $T \\supset F = F$, so second premise false.  \nWait—try $E = T$, $F = T$, $G = F$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = T ⊃ T = T  \n- $G \\supset \\sim F$ = F ⊃ F = T  \n- Conclusion $\\sim G \\vee \\sim E$ = T ∨ F = T  \nAll good.  \nBut option C says: $E = T$, $G = T$, $F = F$. Check:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = T ⊃ F = **F** → premise false → not a counterexample.  \nOption D: $F = T$, $G = T$, $E = F$:  \n- $E \\supset F$ = F ⊃ T = T  \n- $G \\supset \\sim F$ = T ⊃ F = **F** → premise false.  \nOption B: $E = T$, $F = T$, $G = T$:  \n- $E \\supset F = T$, $G \\supset \\sim F = T \\supset F = F$ → second premise false.  \nWait—maybe we misread the conclusion: it’s $\\sim G \\vee \\sim E$, which is logically equivalent to $G \\supset \\sim E$, and from the premises: $E \\supset F$, $G \\supset \\sim F$ ⇒ by hypothetical syllogism, $G \\supset \\sim E$? Indeed: $G \\supset \\sim F$, and $E \\supset F$ ⇒ contrapositive $\\sim F \\supset \\sim E$, so $G \\supset \\sim F$, $\\sim F \\supset \\sim E$ ⇒ $G \\supset \\sim E$, which is $\\sim G \\vee \\sim E$. So the argument **is valid**.  \n✅ Therefore, the correct answer is **A**.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 176,
        "output_tokens": 1011,
        "total_tokens": 1187
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
